// GENERATED by scripts/build-courses.ts from src/lib/courses/content — edit those files, then run: npm run courses
import type { LessonText } from '../types';
const text: LessonText[][] = [[{"say":"America's history didn't start in 1492. Millions of people already lived here in diverse, complex societies.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Native peoples adapted to very different environments, which shaped how they lived:"},{"t":"list","items":["In the Southwest, the spread of maize (corn) farming supported permanent villages, like the Pueblo peoples with irrigation systems.","In the Great Basin and Great Plains, many groups were mobile hunter-gatherers.","In the Northeast and Mississippi River Valley, peoples mixed farming (the \"three sisters\": corn, beans, squash) with hunting and built permanent villages. The Iroquois formed a powerful confederacy.","In the Pacific Northwest and California, rich natural resources supported large, permanent settlements based on fishing and gathering."]},{"t":"key","text":"Native societies were diverse, not one single culture. Their environments shaped their economies, social structures and beliefs."}]},{"say":"When Europeans reached the Americas, two worlds collided. The exchange that followed changed the entire planet.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Europeans sailed west for several reasons, summed up as \"God, gold and glory\": spreading Christianity, finding wealth and trade routes to Asia, and national competition. New technology (the caravel, compass, astrolabe) and strong monarchies made long voyages possible."},{"t":"key","text":"The Columbian Exchange was the transfer of plants, animals, people and diseases between the Eastern and Western Hemispheres after 1492."},{"t":"list","items":["To the Americas: horses, cattle, pigs, wheat, sugar, and diseases like smallpox.","To Europe, Africa and Asia: maize, potatoes, tomatoes, cacao, tobacco."]},{"t":"p","text":"Smallpox and other diseases killed a huge share of the Native population, which had no immunity. American crops led to population growth in Europe, and American silver fueled the European economy, helping shift it toward capitalism."}]},{"say":"Spain built a huge empire in the Americas. Its system shaped society there for centuries.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Spanish conquistadors like Hernán Cortés (Aztecs) and Francisco Pizarro (Inca) conquered powerful empires with help from disease, Native allies and steel weapons."},{"t":"list","items":["The encomienda system gave Spanish colonists the right to Native labor and tribute, leading to brutal exploitation.","As the Native population fell, the Spanish increasingly turned to enslaved Africans, starting the Atlantic slave trade in the Americas.","The casta system ranked people by ancestry: Spanish-born at the top, then American-born Spanish, then mixed-race people, Native peoples and Africans."]},{"t":"key","text":"Debates about how to treat Native peoples, like the Valladolid debate between Bartolomé de las Casas and Juan Ginés de Sepúlveda, showed European views about race and religion."}]}],[{"say":"England's colonies developed very differently depending on where and why they were founded.","blocks":[{"t":"list","items":["Chesapeake (Virginia, Maryland): founded for profit. Jamestown (1607) survived thanks to tobacco. Labor came first from indentured servants, then increasingly enslaved Africans. Bacon's Rebellion (1676) helped push planters toward slavery.","New England (Massachusetts, Connecticut): founded by Puritans seeking a religious community (\"city upon a hill\"). Family farms, towns, town meetings, shipbuilding and trade.","Middle colonies (New York, Pennsylvania): diverse and tolerant. Pennsylvania was founded by Quaker William Penn. Grain exports earned them the name \"breadbasket.\"","Southern colonies (Carolinas, Georgia): plantation economies growing rice and indigo with large enslaved populations."]},{"t":"key","text":"Each region's economy, labor system and society grew from its environment and founders' goals."}]},{"say":"The colonies were part of a huge Atlantic trading system, and slavery was at its center.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Mercantilism held that colonies existed to enrich the mother country by supplying raw materials and buying finished goods. The Navigation Acts required colonial trade to go through English ships and ports."},{"t":"p","text":"The \"triangular trade\" connected Europe, Africa and the Americas: manufactured goods to Africa, enslaved Africans to the Americas (the brutal Middle Passage), and raw materials like sugar, tobacco and rice to Europe."},{"t":"key","text":"Slavery became a race-based, hereditary system by law. Colonial slave codes defined enslaved people as property for life, and the status passed to their children."},{"t":"p","text":"Enslaved Africans resisted in many ways: slowing work, preserving culture and family, running away, and open revolt like the Stono Rebellion (1739) in South Carolina."},{"t":"p","text":"In practice, England's policy of salutary neglect (loose enforcement) let the colonies develop self-government and trade freely for decades."}]},{"say":"By the 1700s, colonists were developing new ideas about religion, reason and self-government.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Great Awakening (1730s–1740s) was a wave of emotional religious revivals. Preachers like Jonathan Edwards and George Whitefield emphasized personal faith over church authority. It encouraged people to question established authority and created new denominations."},{"t":"p","text":"The Enlightenment spread ideas of reason, natural rights and science. Thinkers like John Locke argued government exists to protect life, liberty and property. Benjamin Franklin became a famous American example of Enlightenment thinking."},{"t":"p","text":"Colonists also built self-government: the Virginia House of Burgesses (1619), the Mayflower Compact (1620) and New England town meetings."},{"t":"key","text":"Both the Great Awakening and the Enlightenment encouraged colonists to question authority, which later fed into revolutionary ideas."}]}],[{"say":"Britain won a huge war for North America, but the victory set it on a collision course with its own colonists.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The French and Indian War (1754–1763) was fought between Britain and France (with Native allies) over the Ohio River Valley. Britain won and gained French Canada and land east of the Mississippi."},{"t":"p","text":"But the war left Britain deeply in debt, so it ended salutary neglect and tried to tax the colonies and control them more tightly:"},{"t":"list","items":["Proclamation of 1763: banned colonial settlement west of the Appalachians.","Sugar Act (1764) and Stamp Act (1765): taxes to raise revenue.","Townshend Acts (1767): taxes on imported goods like glass, paper and tea."]},{"t":"key","text":"Colonists protested \"no taxation without representation,\" arguing only their own elected assemblies could tax them."},{"t":"p","text":"Protests included boycotts, the Stamp Act Congress, the Sons of Liberty and, eventually, the Boston Tea Party (1773). Britain responded with the Coercive (Intolerable) Acts in 1774."}]},{"say":"Protest turned into war, and war turned into a fight for independence. Here's how a colonial rebellion became a new nation.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Fighting began at Lexington and Concord in 1775. Thomas Paine's pamphlet Common Sense (1776) persuaded many colonists to support full independence."},{"t":"p","text":"The Declaration of Independence (July 4, 1776), written mainly by Thomas Jefferson, used Enlightenment ideas: all men are created equal with unalienable rights to life, liberty and the pursuit of happiness, and people may overthrow a government that violates those rights."},{"t":"key","text":"The American victory at Saratoga (1777) convinced France to ally with the Americans, a turning point in the war."},{"t":"p","text":"With French help, George Washington's army forced the British surrender at Yorktown (1781). The Treaty of Paris (1783) recognized American independence with land to the Mississippi River."},{"t":"p","text":"The Revolution raised questions it didn't answer: women's roles (\"Republican Motherhood\"), slavery (some Northern states began gradual emancipation), and the rights of Native Americans."}]},{"say":"Winning independence was one challenge. Building a government that worked was another.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Articles of Confederation (1781) created a weak central government: no power to tax, no executive, no national courts, and changes required all 13 states. Shays' Rebellion (1786–87) showed it couldn't keep order."},{"t":"p","text":"At the Constitutional Convention (1787), delegates compromised:"},{"t":"list","items":["Great Compromise: a bicameral Congress with a Senate (equal representation) and a House (by population).","Three-Fifths Compromise: enslaved people counted as three-fifths of a person for representation and taxes.","Separation of powers and checks and balances among three branches, and federalism (power shared between national and state governments)."]},{"t":"key","text":"Federalists (Hamilton, Madison, The Federalist Papers) supported ratification; Anti-Federalists feared a strong central government and demanded a Bill of Rights, added in 1791."},{"t":"p","text":"Under Washington and Adams, the first political parties formed: Hamilton's Federalists (strong national government, national bank, commerce) versus Jefferson's Democratic-Republicans (states' rights, farmers, strict reading of the Constitution). Washington's Farewell Address warned against parties and foreign alliances."}]}],[{"say":"The young nation grew fast in size, in democracy and in conflict.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Jefferson's presidency included the Louisiana Purchase (1803) from France, doubling the nation's size even though the Constitution didn't explicitly allow it. Lewis and Clark explored it."},{"t":"p","text":"The War of 1812 with Britain (over impressment of sailors and frontier conflicts) ended in a draw but boosted nationalism and weakened Native resistance in the Northwest."},{"t":"p","text":"The Supreme Court under John Marshall strengthened federal power: Marbury v. Madison (1803) established judicial review; McCulloch v. Maryland (1819) upheld the national bank."},{"t":"key","text":"Jacksonian democracy expanded voting to most white men by ending property requirements, but excluded women, African Americans and Native Americans."},{"t":"p","text":"Andrew Jackson vetoed the Second Bank of the United States, fought South Carolina over nullification of a tariff, and signed the Indian Removal Act (1830). Despite the Supreme Court's ruling in Worcester v. Georgia, the Cherokee were forced west on the Trail of Tears (1838)."}]},{"say":"Between 1800 and 1848, new technology and transportation turned America into a connected national market.","blocks":[{"t":"list","items":["Transportation revolution: the National Road, canals like the Erie Canal (1825), steamboats and early railroads cut shipping costs dramatically.","Industrialization: textile mills like those in Lowell, Massachusetts, employed young women (\"mill girls\"). The factory system moved production out of homes.","Inventions: Eli Whitney's cotton gin (1793) made cotton hugely profitable and expanded slavery in the South; interchangeable parts; the steel plow and mechanical reaper for Western farms."]},{"t":"key","text":"Regions specialized: the North in manufacturing and commerce, the South in cotton grown by enslaved people, and the West in grain and livestock. This tied regions together economically but deepened differences over slavery."},{"t":"p","text":"Henry Clay's American System proposed tariffs, a national bank and internal improvements to link the regions."},{"t":"p","text":"Immigration from Ireland and Germany grew, sparking nativist (anti-immigrant) reactions."}]},{"say":"The Second Great Awakening inspired Americans to fix society. Meanwhile, many believed the nation was destined to stretch across the continent.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Second Great Awakening encouraged believers to improve themselves and society, fueling reform:"},{"t":"list","items":["Abolitionism: William Lloyd Garrison, Frederick Douglass and Harriet Tubman fought to end slavery.","Women's rights: the Seneca Falls Convention (1848) and its Declaration of Sentiments, led by Elizabeth Cady Stanton and Lucretia Mott.","Temperance (limiting alcohol), public education (Horace Mann) and asylum reform (Dorothea Dix)."]},{"t":"key","text":"Manifest Destiny was the belief that the US was destined to expand across North America."},{"t":"p","text":"The US annexed Texas (1845), split Oregon with Britain, and won the Mexican-American War (1846–1848). The Treaty of Guadalupe Hidalgo gave the US California and the Southwest. The new territory reopened the explosive question: would it allow slavery?"}]}],[{"say":"For decades, compromises held the Union together. In the 1850s, they started to fail.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Key steps toward war:"},{"t":"list","items":["Compromise of 1850: California admitted free; a stronger Fugitive Slave Act angered Northerners.","Uncle Tom's Cabin (1852) turned many Northerners against slavery.","Kansas-Nebraska Act (1854): popular sovereignty let settlers vote on slavery, overturning the Missouri Compromise line and leading to violence in \"Bleeding Kansas.\"","The Republican Party formed to stop the spread of slavery.","Dred Scott v. Sandford (1857): the Supreme Court ruled enslaved people were not citizens and Congress couldn't ban slavery in territories.","John Brown's raid on Harpers Ferry (1859)."]},{"t":"key","text":"Abraham Lincoln's election in 1860 on a platform opposing slavery's expansion led Southern states to secede and form the Confederacy."}]},{"say":"The Civil War was the deadliest war in American history. It began as a war for the Union and became a war to end slavery.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The war began at Fort Sumter (1861). The North had advantages in population, industry and railroads; the South had skilled generals and fought defensively on home ground."},{"t":"key","text":"Lincoln's Emancipation Proclamation (1863) freed enslaved people in Confederate-held areas, made ending slavery a war goal, and kept Britain and France from supporting the Confederacy."},{"t":"p","text":"About 180,000 African American soldiers fought for the Union. The Union victory at Gettysburg (1863) and the capture of Vicksburg (giving control of the Mississippi) were turning points. Sherman's March to the Sea destroyed the South's resources. Lee surrendered at Appomattox in 1865."},{"t":"p","text":"Lincoln's Gettysburg Address redefined the war as a fight for \"a new birth of freedom.\" The 13th Amendment (1865) abolished slavery everywhere in the US."}]},{"say":"After the war, the nation had to rebuild the South and decide what freedom would mean for four million formerly enslaved people.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Lincoln's mild plan was cut short by his assassination. President Andrew Johnson was lenient to former Confederates, and Southern states passed Black Codes restricting freedpeople."},{"t":"p","text":"Radical Republicans in Congress took control:"},{"t":"list","items":["14th Amendment (1868): citizenship and equal protection of the laws for all born in the US.","15th Amendment (1870): voting rights could not be denied based on race.","The Freedmen's Bureau provided food, schools and legal help. African Americans were elected to Congress and state legislatures."]},{"t":"key","text":"Reconstruction ended with the Compromise of 1877, which withdrew federal troops from the South. Afterward, white Southerners imposed Jim Crow segregation and used poll taxes, literacy tests and violence to block Black voting."},{"t":"p","text":"Sharecropping kept many freedpeople in debt and poverty. Groups like the Ku Klux Klan used terror to undo Reconstruction's gains."}]}],[{"say":"After the Civil War, America became the world's largest industrial economy. Big business changed everything.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Railroads, steel, oil and electricity transformed the economy. The transcontinental railroad (1869) linked the coasts."},{"t":"list","items":["Andrew Carnegie (steel) used vertical integration: controlling every step of production.","John D. Rockefeller (Standard Oil) used horizontal integration: buying up competitors to form a monopoly.","J.P. Morgan financed and reorganized major industries."]},{"t":"key","text":"Supporters called them \"captains of industry\"; critics called them \"robber barons.\" Social Darwinism was used to justify great wealth, while the Gospel of Wealth argued the rich should give back."},{"t":"p","text":"The Sherman Antitrust Act (1890) outlawed monopolies but was weakly enforced at first. Mark Twain called the era the \"Gilded Age\": shiny on the surface, with corruption and poverty underneath."}]},{"say":"Factories needed workers, and millions came from around the world. Cities exploded, and workers fought for fair treatment.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"\"New immigrants\" from Southern and Eastern Europe (Italy, Poland, Russia) and Asia arrived, many through Ellis Island and Angel Island. Nativists pushed back: the Chinese Exclusion Act (1882) banned Chinese immigration."},{"t":"p","text":"Cities grew rapidly, with crowded tenements, disease and political machines (like Tammany Hall) that traded services for votes. Jane Addams's Hull House offered help to immigrants."},{"t":"key","text":"Workers organized to fight long hours, low pay and dangerous conditions. The Knights of Labor welcomed all workers; the American Federation of Labor (AFL), led by Samuel Gompers, organized skilled workers for practical gains."},{"t":"p","text":"Major strikes, like the Great Railroad Strike (1877), Haymarket (1886), Homestead (1892) and Pullman (1894), were often crushed with government help."}]},{"say":"Settlers, railroads and the army pushed into the West, with devastating results for Native nations. Farmers also rose up.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Homestead Act (1862) offered free land to settlers. Railroads, mining and cattle ranching drew people west."},{"t":"p","text":"Native nations resisted, including at the Battle of Little Bighorn (1876), but the destruction of the buffalo and military force confined them to reservations. The Dawes Act (1887) broke up reservations into individual plots to force assimilation, and Native people lost much of their land. The Wounded Knee Massacre (1890) marked the violent end of armed resistance."},{"t":"key","text":"Struggling farmers formed the Grange and then the Populist (People's) Party. The Omaha Platform (1892) called for free coinage of silver, a graduated income tax, government ownership of railroads and direct election of senators."},{"t":"p","text":"The Populists faded after William Jennings Bryan lost the 1896 election, but many of their ideas later became law."}]}],[{"say":"Around 1900, America looked outward to build an empire and inward to fix the problems of industrial society.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The US gained an overseas empire after the Spanish-American War (1898): the Philippines, Puerto Rico and Guam. It annexed Hawaii and built the Panama Canal. Supporters cited markets and national power; anti-imperialists argued it betrayed American ideals."},{"t":"key","text":"Progressives used government to fix problems caused by industrialization, urbanization and corruption."},{"t":"list","items":["Muckrakers like Upton Sinclair (The Jungle) and Ida Tarbell exposed abuses.","Laws included the Meat Inspection Act and Pure Food and Drug Act (1906).","Amendments: 16th (income tax), 17th (direct election of senators), 18th (Prohibition), 19th (women's suffrage, 1920).","Presidents Theodore Roosevelt (trust-busting, conservation), Taft and Wilson (Federal Reserve, Clayton Antitrust Act)."]},{"t":"p","text":"W.E.B. Du Bois and the NAACP fought for civil rights, while Booker T. Washington emphasized economic self-help."}]},{"say":"America joined the Great War late but emerged as a world power. Then came a decade of change and conflict at home.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The US entered World War I in 1917 after German submarine attacks and the Zimmermann Telegram. At home, the government limited dissent (Espionage and Sedition Acts) and the Great Migration of African Americans to Northern cities began."},{"t":"p","text":"Wilson's Fourteen Points proposed a League of Nations, but the Senate rejected the Treaty of Versailles, and the US stayed out of the League."},{"t":"key","text":"The 1920s brought consumer culture (cars, radio, buying on credit) and cultural clashes between modern and traditional values."},{"t":"list","items":["The Harlem Renaissance celebrated African American art, music and literature (Langston Hughes, jazz).","The First Red Scare targeted immigrants and radicals.","Immigration quotas (1924) sharply limited Southern and Eastern Europeans.","The Scopes Trial debated teaching evolution; Prohibition fueled organized crime; the KKK revived."]}]},{"say":"The economy collapsed in 1929, the government took on new roles, and then the nation fought a world war.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The stock market crash of 1929 began the Great Depression, caused by overproduction, unequal wealth, buying stocks on credit, and bank failures. Unemployment reached about 25%. Hoover's limited response was unpopular."},{"t":"key","text":"Franklin Roosevelt's New Deal used government to provide relief, recovery and reform, permanently expanding the federal government's role."},{"t":"list","items":["Relief: jobs programs like the CCC and WPA.","Reform: the FDIC insured bank deposits; the SEC regulated stocks; Social Security (1935) created old-age pensions; the Wagner Act protected unions."]},{"t":"p","text":"World War II: after Pearl Harbor (December 7, 1941), the US entered the war. Wartime production ended the Depression. Women and minorities filled factory jobs (\"Rosie the Riveter\"), but Japanese Americans were forced into internment camps (upheld in Korematsu v. United States). The war ended in 1945 after the atomic bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki."}]}],[{"say":"After World War II, the US and Soviet Union became rivals in a global struggle that lasted over 40 years.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Containment was the US policy of stopping the spread of communism."},{"t":"list","items":["Truman Doctrine (1947): aid to nations resisting communism.","Marshall Plan: billions to rebuild Western Europe.","NATO (1949): a military alliance against the Soviets.","The Berlin Airlift supplied West Berlin during a Soviet blockade."]},{"t":"p","text":"Hot wars broke out in Korea (1950–53) and later Vietnam. The nuclear arms race brought the world to the brink during the Cuban Missile Crisis (1962). The space race followed Sputnik (1957)."},{"t":"p","text":"At home, the Second Red Scare led to McCarthyism: Senator Joseph McCarthy's reckless accusations of communist infiltration."}]},{"say":"African Americans led a movement that challenged segregation and transformed American law and society.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Brown v. Board of Education (1954) ruled that segregated public schools were unconstitutional, overturning \"separate but equal\" from Plessy v. Ferguson (1896)."},{"t":"list","items":["The Montgomery Bus Boycott (1955–56), sparked by Rosa Parks and led by Martin Luther King Jr., used nonviolent protest.","Sit-ins, Freedom Rides and the March on Washington (1963) pressured the nation.","The Civil Rights Act of 1964 banned segregation in public places and job discrimination.","The Voting Rights Act of 1965 outlawed literacy tests and brought federal oversight of voting."]},{"t":"p","text":"Later, some activists like Malcolm X and the Black Power movement emphasized self-defense, pride and self-reliance."},{"t":"p","text":"The movement inspired others: the women's movement (NOW, debates over the Equal Rights Amendment), the Chicano movement (César Chávez and farmworkers), the American Indian Movement, and the gay rights movement after Stonewall (1969)."}]},{"say":"The 1960s brought ambitious reforms, a divisive war, and a loss of trust in government that lasted into the 1970s.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Lyndon Johnson's Great Society launched a War on Poverty, Medicare and Medicaid, federal aid to education, and the Immigration Act of 1965, which ended national-origin quotas."},{"t":"key","text":"The Vietnam War deeply divided the nation. After the Gulf of Tonkin Resolution (1964), Johnson escalated the war. The Tet Offensive (1968) shook public confidence, and a large antiwar movement grew, especially among students."},{"t":"p","text":"Nixon pursued \"Vietnamization\" and détente with the Soviet Union and China. The War Powers Act (1973) tried to limit presidential war-making."},{"t":"p","text":"The Watergate scandal led Nixon to resign in 1974. The 1970s brought stagflation (inflation plus unemployment), an energy crisis, and growing environmental action (the EPA, Clean Air Act)."}]}],[{"say":"In 1980, a conservative movement swept Ronald Reagan into office, and within a decade, the Cold War was over.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The conservative resurgence combined free-market economics, a strong anti-communist foreign policy, and the religious right's focus on traditional values."},{"t":"key","text":"Reaganomics cut taxes and regulations (supply-side economics) while increasing military spending, which also led to large budget deficits."},{"t":"p","text":"Reagan took a hard line against the Soviet Union, building up the military and proposing the Strategic Defense Initiative. Later he negotiated arms reductions with Soviet leader Mikhail Gorbachev."},{"t":"p","text":"The Berlin Wall fell in 1989, and the Soviet Union collapsed in 1991, ending the Cold War and leaving the US as the world's only superpower."}]},{"say":"Since 1990, technology and global trade have reshaped the economy, while new challenges tested the nation.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Globalization connected economies through trade agreements like NAFTA (1994). Manufacturing jobs shifted overseas, while the economy moved toward services and technology. The internet and digital technology transformed work, communication and culture."},{"t":"p","text":"The population became more diverse through immigration, especially from Latin America and Asia, sparking debates about immigration policy."},{"t":"key","text":"The September 11, 2001 terrorist attacks led to wars in Afghanistan and Iraq, the creation of the Department of Homeland Security, and debates over security versus civil liberties (the Patriot Act)."},{"t":"p","text":"The Great Recession (2008) followed a housing and financial crisis. Ongoing debates involve health care, the role of government, climate change, and economic inequality."}]}]];
export default text;
